{
  "id": "sceneggiata_napoletana",
  "name": "Sceneggiata (Neapolitan song melodrama)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "The sceneggiata is a Neapolitan popular stage form in which a well-known song is expanded into a short melodrama of love, betrayal, honour and revenge, spoken scenes building up to the song itself and played out by the stock figures of hero, heroine and villain (isso, essa e 'o malamente). It arose in Naples around the end of the First World War and was revived in the 1970s by Mario Merola, whose stage shows, records and films made it popular again, followed by singers such as Mario Trevi and Nino D'Angelo.",
  "recipe": "classic Southern European mid-1970s sceneggiata, ballad Poetry, Mediterranean ballad branch. single lead single track no stacking. bowl back Italian Italian alpine spruce brescian calace mandolin, classical nylon-string guitar, violin orchestral. naples canzone napoletana defining 1839-present mid tier naples canzone napoletana studio. Neumann U67, tube pre, colored vintage console, vari-mu, Pultec passive program EQ. tape 15ips echo plate reverb.",
